-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 1: Modbus RTU and TCP Integration for BAS
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'how-to'),
  'Modbus RTU and TCP Integration for Building Automation Systems',
  'modbus-rtu-tcp-integration-building-automation',
  'Practical guide to integrating Modbus RTU (serial) and Modbus TCP (Ethernet) devices into BAS, covering register types, addressing conventions, byte order, scaling, and common troubleshooting patterns.',
  E'# Modbus RTU and TCP Integration for Building Automation Systems\n\nModbus remains one of the most widely used communication protocols in building automation, particularly for power meters, variable frequency drives, boilers, and third-party sensors. Despite its age, Modbus is found in new installations because many device manufacturers continue to support it and it is straightforward to implement at the field level.\n\n## Protocol Variants\n\nThe Modbus protocol has two primary variants used in BAS:\n\n| Variant | Transport | Speed | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Modbus RTU | RS-485 serial | 9600–115200 baud | Meters, VFDs, boilers, legacy sensors |\n| Modbus TCP | Ethernet (TCP/IP) | Network speed | Power meters, gateways, newer equipment |\n\nModbus RTU uses a compact binary encoding and communicates over RS-485 serial lines. Modbus TCP wraps the same application-layer protocol in TCP/IP packets, removing the need for serial wiring and CRC calculation.\n\nA third variant, Modbus ASCII, exists but is rarely used in BAS applications.\n\n## Register Types\n\nThe Modbus data model defines four primary register types, each with a specific address range as defined in the Modbus Application Protocol Specification:\n\n| Register Type | Data Size | Access | Classic Address Range | Function Codes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Coil | 1 bit | Read/Write | 00001–09999 | FC01 (read), FC05/FC15 (write) |\n| Discrete Input | 1 bit | Read Only | 10001–19999 | FC02 |\n| Input Register | 16 bits | Read Only | 30001–39999 | FC04 |\n| Holding Register | 16 bits | Read/Write | 40001–49999 | FC03 (read), FC06/FC16 (write) |\n\n## Addressing: 0-Based vs 1-Based\n\nOne of the most common sources of Modbus integration errors is the addressing offset convention. The Modbus protocol itself uses 0-based addressing in the wire protocol (the Protocol Data Unit). However, many device manuals and BAS configuration tools use 1-based register numbers from the traditional Modbus data model.\n\nFor example, Holding Register 40001 in the 1-based convention corresponds to protocol address 0 (zero) in function code 03. Some BAS platforms automatically subtract 1; others expect the raw protocol address. Always confirm which convention your BAS platform and the device documentation use before mapping registers.\n\n## Byte Order and Word Order\n\n16-bit Modbus registers store data in big-endian (most significant byte first) order as specified in the Modbus standard. However, when 32-bit values (such as floating-point IEEE 754 numbers or 32-bit integers) span two consecutive registers, the order of the two registers varies by manufacturer:\n\n| Word Order | Register Layout | Common Names |\n|---|---|---|\n| Big-endian | High word first, low word second | AB CD |\n| Little-endian | Low word first, high word second | CD AB |\n| Byte-swapped big | High word first, bytes swapped | BA DC |\n| Byte-swapped little | Low word first, bytes swapped | DC BA |\n\nIf a 32-bit floating-point value reads as an implausible number (extremely large, extremely small, or NaN), the word order or byte order is likely wrong. Consult the device manufacturer documentation for the specific register map and byte order.\n\n## Scaling and Engineering Units\n\nMany Modbus devices transmit raw integer values that require scaling to engineering units. Common patterns:\n\n| Device Reads | Scaling | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2345 | Divide by 10 | 234.5 degF |\n| 4820 | Divide by 100 | 48.20 kW |\n| 1 | Multiply by 0.1 | 0.1 psi |\n\nThe device register map documentation specifies the scaling factor and engineering units for each register. Do not assume a scaling factor based on another device from the same manufacturer, as it may differ between models or firmware versions.\n\n## RS-485 Wiring for Modbus RTU\n\nModbus RTU communicates over RS-485 serial lines. Correct wiring is essential for reliable communication:\n\n- **Topology**: Daisy chain (bus topology). Avoid star wiring and T-taps.\n- **Cable**: Shielded twisted pair. Use one pair for data (A/B or +/-) and connect shield at one end only to avoid ground loops.\n- **Termination**: 120-ohm termination resistors at both physical ends of the bus.\n- **Polarity**: Maintain consistent A/B polarity across all devices. Some manufacturers label the positive data line as A, others as B.\n- **Maximum devices**: The RS-485 specification (TIA/EIA-485) supports up to 32 unit loads per segment without repeaters.\n- **Maximum distance**: 1200 meters (4000 feet) at lower baud rates per TIA/EIA-485.\n\n## Modbus TCP Considerations\n\n- Each Modbus TCP device has an IP address and listens on TCP port 502 (the IANA-assigned port for Modbus).\n- Unit ID in the TCP header is typically 1 for direct connections, but is significant when communicating through a Modbus TCP-to-RTU gateway (it maps to the RTU slave address).\n- Multiple simultaneous TCP connections may be limited by the device. Check the manufacturer specification for the maximum number of concurrent connections.\n\n## Common Integration Patterns in BAS\n\n| Device Type | Typical Registers | Protocol Variant |\n|---|---|---|\n| Power meter | Voltage, current, power, energy, power factor | Modbus TCP or RTU |\n| VFD | Speed command, speed feedback, status, fault codes | Modbus RTU or TCP |\n| Boiler controller | Supply temp, firing rate, status, setpoint | Modbus RTU |\n| Weather station | Temperature, humidity, wind, solar radiation | Modbus RTU |\n| Gas/water meter | Totalized flow, instantaneous rate | Modbus RTU |\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| No response from device | Wrong slave address, baud rate mismatch, wiring | Verify address, baud rate, parity, and wiring |\n| CRC errors (RTU) | Noise, wrong baud rate, termination | Check wiring, shielding, termination, baud rate |\n| Values read as zero or max | Wrong register address | Verify 0-based vs 1-based offset |\n| Floating-point reads as garbage | Wrong byte/word order | Try all four byte-order combinations |\n| Intermittent timeouts | Polling too fast, bus overloaded | Increase polling interval, reduce device count |\n| Gateway returns exception code | Unsupported function code or address | Verify function code and register range in device docs |\n\n### Modbus Exception Codes\n\nWhen a device rejects a request, it returns an exception response. Common exception codes as defined in the Modbus Application Protocol Specification:\n\n| Code | Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 01 | Illegal Function | The function code is not supported by the device |\n| 02 | Illegal Data Address | The register address is out of range |\n| 03 | Illegal Data Value | The value in the request is not acceptable |\n| 04 | Server Device Failure | The device encountered an internal error |\n| 06 | Server Device Busy | The device is busy processing another request |\n\n## Polling Best Practices\n\n- Start with a polling interval of 5–15 seconds per device and adjust based on the application requirement and controller load.\n- Group consecutive registers into single read requests where possible to reduce the number of transactions.\n- Avoid polling non-existent registers; some devices respond slowly or with errors when queried for unsupported addresses.\n- Monitor BAS controller CPU utilization when adding Modbus integrations; each serial poll is a blocking operation on RTU.\n\n## References\n\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus over Serial Line Specification and Implementation Guide V1.02](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [IANA - Service Name and Transport Protocol Port Number Registry (Port 502)](https://www.iana.org/assignments/service-names-port-numbers/service-names-port-numbers.xhtml)\n- [TIA/EIA-485 Standard - Electrical Characteristics of Generators and Receivers for Balanced Digital Multipoint Systems](https://www.tiaonline.org/)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

