-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 2: BACnet Priority Arrays and Commandable Objects
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'BACnet Priority Arrays and Commandable Objects',
  'bacnet-priority-arrays-commandable-objects',
  'Reference guide to BACnet priority arrays, the 16-level command priority scheme, relinquish defaults, and common troubleshooting patterns for override conflicts in building automation systems.',
  E'# BACnet Priority Arrays and Commandable Objects\n\nThe priority array is the mechanism BACnet uses to resolve conflicting commands from multiple sources writing to the same output. Every commandable object (Analog Output, Analog Value, Binary Output, Binary Value, and others designated as commandable) contains a priority array with 16 slots, numbered 1 (highest) through 16 (lowest). Understanding this mechanism is essential for diagnosing override conflicts, stuck outputs, and unexpected equipment behavior.\n\n## The 16 Priority Levels\n\nASHRAE Standard 135 defines the following priority levels for the BACnet priority array:\n\n| Priority | Name | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Manual-Life Safety | Fire, smoke control, stairwell pressurization |\n| 2 | Automatic-Life Safety | Smoke damper control, fire suppression interlocks |\n| 3 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 4 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 5 | Critical Equipment Control | Chiller safeties, freeze protection |\n| 6 | Minimum On/Off | Minimum runtime protection |\n| 7 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 8 | Manual Operator | Operator overrides from front-end or field devices |\n| 9 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 10 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 11 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 12 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 13 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 14 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 15 | Available | Application-specific |\n| 16 | Available | Application-specific (often used as the default scheduling or normal control priority) |\n\nThe names for priorities 1, 2, 5, 6, and 8 are specified by the standard. Priorities 3, 4, and 7–16 are available for application use, though industry convention often assigns specific roles to certain levels.\n\n## How the Priority Array Works\n\nWhen multiple sources write to the same commandable object at different priority levels:\n\n1. The object evaluates all 16 slots in the priority array.\n2. The highest-priority (lowest-numbered) slot that contains a value wins.\n3. That value becomes the present value of the object.\n4. All lower-priority values remain in their slots but have no effect until higher-priority values are released.\n\nWhen a source releases (relinquishes) its command, it writes a NULL to its priority slot. The object then falls through to the next highest-priority slot that contains a value.\n\n## Relinquish Default\n\nIf all 16 priority slots are NULL (no active commands), the object uses its **Relinquish_Default** property as the present value. This property acts as a safety net to ensure the object always has a defined state.\n\nFor a Binary Output, the relinquish default is typically configured to the safe state for the controlled equipment (for example, OFF for a fan or CLOSED for a valve). For an Analog Output, it is typically configured to a safe value such as 0% or a known-safe position.\n\n## Writing Commands at a Specific Priority\n\nBACnet WriteProperty and WritePropertyMultiple services include a priority parameter. When writing to a commandable object:\n\n- Specify both the value and the priority level.\n- The value is placed in the specified priority slot.\n- If a higher-priority slot already contains a value, the write succeeds (the value is stored) but does not change the present value.\n\nTo release a command, write NULL to the specific priority slot.\n\n## Common Priority Conventions in BAS Projects\n\nWhile the standard only mandates names for a few levels, many BAS projects follow conventions like these:\n\n| Priority | Common Convention |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Life safety (reserved for fire/smoke systems) |\n| 5 | Critical equipment protection |\n| 8 | Manual operator overrides |\n| 9 | Emergency or demand-response commands |\n| 12 | Scheduling (occupied/unoccupied mode) |\n| 14 | Optimization routines (trim-and-respond, load shedding) |\n| 16 | Normal control loop output |\n\nThese conventions vary by project and BAS vendor. Document the priority assignment scheme in the project sequence of operations.\n\n## Troubleshooting Priority Array Issues\n\n### Output Stuck at an Unexpected Value\n\nThe most common priority array issue is an output that appears stuck and does not respond to normal control commands.\n\n**Diagnosis:** Read the priority array of the commandable object. Look for a non-NULL value at a higher priority than the control loop expects to write.\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Output stuck ON or at a fixed value | A higher-priority slot holds a command that was never released | Write NULL to the stuck priority slot |\n| Output does not respond to operator override (P8) | A life safety or equipment protection command (P1–P6) is active | Investigate and resolve the safety condition |\n| Output toggles unexpectedly | Two sources writing at the same priority or adjacent priorities | Audit all writers and assign distinct priority levels |\n| Output goes to relinquish default unexpectedly | All priority slots were released simultaneously | Verify scheduling and control loop logic |\n\n### Reading the Priority Array\n\nTo diagnose command conflicts, read the Priority_Array property of the object. This returns all 16 slots. Slots containing NULL have no active command; slots with a value indicate an active command at that priority.\n\nMost BAS front-end tools provide a way to view the priority array. In Niagara, the priority array is visible in the property sheet of a writable object. In Metasys, it can be inspected through the object properties or advanced diagnostics.\n\n### Preventing Stuck Overrides\n\n- Always pair a write command with a release mechanism (timer-based auto-release or explicit NULL write).\n- Avoid writing at priority 1 or 2 from normal control applications. Reserve these for genuine life safety systems.\n- Document which systems write to each object and at which priority level.\n- Implement override duration limits: when an operator writes at priority 8, automatically release after a configurable timeout (for example, 2, 4, or 8 hours).\n\n## Commandable Object Types\n\nNot all BACnet objects support the priority array. The following standard object types are defined as commandable in ASHRAE 135:\n\n| Object Type | Data Type | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Analog Output (AO) | Floating-point | Valve/damper position, VFD speed command |\n| Analog Value (AV) | Floating-point | Setpoints, calculated values |\n| Binary Output (BO) | Binary (active/inactive) | Fan start/stop, pump on/off |\n| Binary Value (BV) | Binary (active/inactive) | Mode flags, enable/disable |\n| Multi-State Output (MSO) | Unsigned integer | Stage selection, mode selection |\n| Multi-State Value (MSV) | Unsigned integer | Mode state, operating mode |\n\nInput objects (AI, BI, MSI) are not commandable and do not have priority arrays.\n\n## Priority Array and BACnet/SC\n\nBACnet Secure Connect (BACnet/SC) does not change the priority array mechanism. The priority array works identically over BACnet/IP and BACnet/SC transports. The security layer encrypts and authenticates the communication but does not alter the application-layer command priority logic.\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135-2020: BACnet - A Data Communication Protocol for Building Automation and Control Networks](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [BACnet International - BACnet Overview](https://bacnet.org/bacnet-overview/)\n- [ASHRAE SSPC 135 - Standing Standard Project Committee for BACnet](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/standards-and-guidelines)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

