-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 3: Implementing ASHRAE Guideline 36 Sequences in BAS
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'Implementing ASHRAE Guideline 36 Sequences in Building Automation',
  'ashrae-guideline-36-sequences-building-automation',
  'Overview of ASHRAE Guideline 36 high-performance HVAC sequences of operation, including trim-and-respond control, key sequence categories, BAS point requirements, and implementation considerations.',
  E'# Implementing ASHRAE Guideline 36 Sequences in Building Automation\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36, titled "High-Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems," provides standardized, tested control sequences for common HVAC system types. Published by ASHRAE, it aims to improve energy efficiency, occupant comfort, and control system consistency across projects by replacing ad hoc sequences with well-documented, peer-reviewed alternatives.\n\n## What Guideline 36 Covers\n\nGuideline 36-2021 provides sequences of operation for the following system types, among others:\n\n- Multiple-zone variable air volume (VAV) air handling units\n- Single-zone VAV air handling units\n- VAV terminal units (cooling-only, reheat, fan-powered)\n- Dual-duct air handling units and terminal units\n- Chilled water plant control (chillers, pumps, towers)\n- Hot water plant control (boilers, pumps)\n- Zone-level sequences (occupied, unoccupied, setpoint management)\n\nThe guideline does not mandate a specific BAS vendor or programming language. It is written as a specification that a controls contractor implements in whatever platform the project requires.\n\n## Trim-and-Respond Logic\n\nOne of the defining features of Guideline 36 sequences is the use of **trim-and-respond** control logic for resetting supply air temperature, duct static pressure, and other setpoints. This replaces traditional PID-based reset and fixed-schedule reset.\n\nHow trim-and-respond works:\n\n1. A setpoint (for example, supply air temperature or duct static pressure) starts at an initial value.\n2. At a fixed time interval (for example, every 2 minutes), the system evaluates zone requests.\n3. If all zones are satisfied (no requests), the setpoint is trimmed (moved toward a more energy-efficient value) by a fixed trim amount.\n4. If one or more zones have unmet requests, the setpoint is adjusted in the opposite direction (responded) by a fixed respond amount multiplied by the number of requests, up to a maximum respond amount per interval.\n5. The setpoint is bounded by configurable minimum and maximum limits.\n\nThis approach automatically finds the most efficient operating point for current conditions without requiring manual tuning of reset schedules.\n\n### Trim-and-Respond Parameters\n\nThe guideline specifies parameters for each application. For example, for a VAV AHU duct static pressure setpoint reset:\n\n| Parameter | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Device | VAV AHU |\n| SP0 | Initial setpoint (typically the design duct static pressure) |\n| SPmin | Minimum setpoint (lowest allowable static pressure) |\n| SPmax | Maximum setpoint (design static pressure) |\n| Td | Delay on device start before trim-and-respond begins |\n| T | Time step between evaluations |\n| I | Number of ignored requests (deadband) |\n| R | Number of zone requests triggering a respond |\n| SPtrim | Trim amount per time step |\n| SPres | Respond amount per request |\n| SPres-max | Maximum respond amount per time step |\n\n## Key Sequence Categories\n\n### AHU Supply Air Temperature Reset\n\nThe supply air temperature setpoint is reset based on zone cooling and heating requests. When zones are satisfied, the setpoint rises (saving cooling energy). When zones need more cooling, the setpoint drops. This replaces fixed schedules or outdoor-air-based reset.\n\n### AHU Duct Static Pressure Reset\n\nDuct static pressure is trimmed down when VAV boxes are not demanding high airflow and responded up when boxes cannot meet their setpoints. This reduces fan energy significantly compared to a fixed static pressure setpoint.\n\n### VAV Terminal Unit Sequences\n\nGuideline 36 defines detailed sequences for VAV box operation, including:\n\n- Active heating and cooling setpoints with deadband\n- Occupied, unoccupied, and standby modes\n- Zone temperature setpoint adjustment ranges\n- Minimum airflow requirements for ventilation\n- Reheat control sequences for VAV-reheat boxes\n- Fan control for fan-powered terminal units\n\n### Chilled Water Plant Sequences\n\nPlant-level sequences include chiller staging, condenser water temperature reset, chilled water supply temperature reset, and pump staging. These sequences coordinate multiple pieces of equipment to optimize plant efficiency rather than controlling each device independently.\n\n## BAS Point Requirements\n\nImplementing Guideline 36 requires more points and more computational capability than simplified control sequences. Typical additional requirements include:\n\n| Requirement | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Zone-level request logic | Each zone calculates heating and cooling requests based on its deviation from setpoint |\n| Request aggregation | The AHU sequence counts requests from all zones to drive trim-and-respond |\n| Setpoint tracking | The BAS must store and update dynamic setpoints at regular intervals |\n| Mode determination | The system determines occupied, unoccupied, warmup, cooldown, and setback modes |\n| Outdoor air monitoring | Sequences reference outdoor air temperature and humidity for economizer and plant decisions |\n| Timer and scheduling | Trim-and-respond requires stable timers and consistent execution intervals |\n\n## Implementation Considerations\n\n### Naming and Documentation\n\nBecause Guideline 36 sequences reference specific parameters (SP0, SPmin, T, SPtrim, etc.), the BAS programming should use naming that traces back to the guideline section numbers. This makes commissioning, troubleshooting, and future modifications significantly easier.\n\n### Commissioning\n\nGuideline 36 sequences should be commissioned by verifying:\n\n- Each zone correctly generates heating and cooling requests\n- Trim-and-respond parameters match the specified values\n- Setpoints move as expected under varying load conditions\n- Mode transitions (occupied, unoccupied, warmup, cooldown) trigger correctly\n- Safety interlocks (freeze protection, high static pressure) override sequences correctly\n\n### When Not to Use Guideline 36\n\nGuideline 36 is a guideline, not a mandatory standard. The sequences are designed for typical commercial HVAC systems. Systems with unusual requirements (laboratory exhaust, cleanroom pressurization, data center cooling) may need custom sequences that depart from the guideline. The design engineer makes this determination.\n\n## ASHRAE Standard 231P\n\nASHRAE is developing Standard 231P to formalize Guideline 36 sequences in a machine-readable format. This standard aims to allow BAS vendors to import standardized sequences directly rather than requiring manual programming from written descriptions.\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 36-2021: High-Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/guideline-36-high-performance-sequences-of-operation-for-hvac-systems)\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135-2020: BACnet](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [ASHRAE Standard 231P: Control Sequences for Building Commissioning and Automation (in development)](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/standards-and-guidelines)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

