-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 4: BAS Alarm Management and Rationalization
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'BAS Alarm Management and Rationalization',
  'bas-alarm-management-rationalization',
  'Best practices for managing alarms in building automation systems, covering alarm classification, priority assignment, rationalization processes, and strategies to reduce alarm fatigue based on ISA-18.2 and industry guidance.',
  E'# BAS Alarm Management and Rationalization\n\nAlarm floods and nuisance alarms are among the most persistent operational problems in building automation. When operators receive hundreds or thousands of alarms per day, they stop investigating individual alarms and critical conditions get missed. Alarm rationalization is the process of reviewing every alarm in the system, assigning appropriate priorities, and eliminating alarms that do not require operator action.\n\n## The Alarm Fatigue Problem\n\nAlarm fatigue occurs when operators are exposed to so many alarms that they become desensitized. Common causes in BAS:\n\n- Every point has an alarm by default, regardless of whether operator action is needed\n- Alarm setpoints are too tight, generating alarms during normal transients\n- Nuisance alarms (known conditions, informational messages) are mixed with critical alarms\n- Alarm priorities are all set to the same level\n- Equipment cycling generates repeated start/stop alarms\n- Network communication faults generate alarms for every affected point\n\nThe ISA-18.2 standard (Management of Alarm Systems for the Process Industries) provides a framework for alarm management that is applicable to building automation, even though it was originally developed for process industries.\n\n## Alarm Classification\n\nNot every abnormal condition needs an alarm. ISA-18.2 distinguishes between:\n\n| Classification | Definition | Operator Action Required |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alarm | An audible and/or visible indication of an abnormal condition requiring operator response | Yes |\n| Alert | A notification that does not require immediate operator action | No (informational) |\n| Event | A recorded occurrence for logging and analysis | No |\n\nMany conditions currently configured as alarms in BAS should be reclassified as alerts or events. For example, a filter pressure differential that is approaching the change threshold is an alert. The filter differential exceeding the threshold and requiring a work order is an alarm.\n\n## Priority Assignment\n\nAlarm priorities should reflect the consequence of failing to respond, not the frequency of occurrence. A common priority scheme aligned with ISA-18.2 principles:\n\n| Priority | Criteria | BAS Examples | Expected Response Time |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Critical | Life safety risk, regulatory violation, major equipment damage | Fire/smoke events, refrigerant leak detection, freeze protection failure | Immediate |\n| High | Significant equipment damage or major comfort impact if not addressed | Chiller plant fault, boiler lockout, total loss of ventilation | Within 30 minutes |\n| Medium | Equipment degradation or moderate comfort impact | AHU fan failure (redundancy available), zone temperature 5+ degrees from setpoint | Within 2 hours |\n| Low | Minor equipment or comfort issue, can wait for next maintenance window | Filter change needed, sensor out of calibration, communication intermittent | Within 24 hours |\n\n## The Rationalization Process\n\nAlarm rationalization reviews every configured alarm against a consistent set of criteria. For each alarm:\n\n### Step 1: Is This an Alarm?\n\nDoes this condition require an operator to take a specific action? If no, reclassify as an alert, event, or remove it.\n\n### Step 2: What Is the Consequence?\n\nWhat happens if the operator does not respond? The consequence determines the priority.\n\n### Step 3: What Is the Operator Response?\n\nEvery alarm should have a documented response procedure. If no one can describe what the operator should do, the alarm likely should not exist.\n\n### Step 4: Are the Setpoints Correct?\n\nAlarm setpoints should be outside the normal operating range with enough margin to avoid nuisance trips during normal transients. Consider:\n\n- Normal operating range for the measurement\n- Process variability and startup/shutdown transients\n- Deadband to prevent alarm chatter (rapid on/off cycling)\n- Delay timers to filter out brief excursions\n\n### Step 5: Is the Priority Correct?\n\nApply the priority scheme consistently. Do not assign high priority to avoid alarms being ignored in a flood of low-priority alarms; fix the flood instead.\n\n## Reducing Alarm Floods\n\nCommon techniques to reduce alarm volume:\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Deadband | Require the value to return past the setpoint by a configurable margin before the alarm can re-trigger |\n| Delay timer | Require the condition to persist for a configurable duration before generating the alarm |\n| State-based suppression | Suppress alarms that are expected during certain operating modes (e.g., startup) |\n| Shelving | Temporarily suppress a known alarm for a fixed duration (with audit trail) |\n| Grouping | For communication faults, generate one parent alarm instead of per-point alarms |\n| Bad-actor analysis | Track the most frequent alarms and address root causes |\n\n## Alarm Routing\n\nNot every alarm needs to go to every operator. Alarm routing should consider:\n\n- Who is responsible for responding to this specific type of alarm?\n- What is the escalation path if the primary responder does not acknowledge?\n- Should after-hours alarms route to a different group or notification method?\n- Do some alarms need to generate work orders in a maintenance management system?\n\n## Ongoing Alarm Management\n\nRationalization is not a one-time event. Ongoing practices include:\n\n| Activity | Frequency |\n|---|---|\n| Review top-10 most frequent alarms | Monthly |\n| Review unacknowledged or standing alarms | Weekly |\n| Rationalize alarms for new equipment or sequences | At commissioning |\n| Audit alarm suppression and shelving | Quarterly |\n| Review alarm response documentation | Annually |\n| Track alarm rate KPIs (alarms per operator per day) | Continuously |\n\nISA-18.2 recommends a target alarm rate of no more than approximately six alarms per operator per hour during normal operation, with manageable peaks during upsets.\n\n## Alarm KPIs\n\nKey performance indicators for alarm system health:\n\n| KPI | Target | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alarms per day | Decreasing trend after rationalization | Alarm log analysis |\n| Percentage of standing alarms | Below 5% of total configured alarms | ISA-18.2 guidance |\n| Stale alarms (active > 24 hours without action) | Minimize | Alarm log analysis |\n| Chattering alarms (>5 trips per day) | Zero or near-zero | Bad-actor analysis |\n| Percentage with documented response | 100% | Rationalization review |\n\n## References\n\n- [ISA-18.2-2016: Management of Alarm Systems for the Process Industries](https://www.isa.org/products/ansi-isa-18-2-2016-management-of-alarm-systems-for)\n- [ISA Technical Report ISA-TR18.2.6: Alarm Systems for Batch and Discrete Processes](https://www.isa.org/)\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 13-2015: Specifying Building Automation Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore)\n- [NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3: Guide to Operational Technology Security](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

