-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 5: Integrating VFDs with BAS Over BACnet and Modbus
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'how-to'),
  'Integrating Variable Frequency Drives with BAS Over BACnet and Modbus',
  'integrating-vfds-bas-bacnet-modbus',
  'Field guide for integrating variable frequency drives (VFDs) into building automation systems using BACnet and Modbus, covering command ownership, speed references, fault handling, and commissioning verification.',
  E'# Integrating Variable Frequency Drives with BAS Over BACnet and Modbus\n\nVariable frequency drives (VFDs) are standard equipment in modern HVAC systems, controlling fans, pumps, and compressors. Integrating VFDs with the BAS over a communication network (rather than hardwired analog signals) provides richer data, simpler wiring, and better diagnostics. However, network-controlled VFDs also introduce complexity around command ownership, safeties, and failure modes that must be addressed deliberately.\n\n## Communication Options\n\n| Protocol | Connection | Typical Setup |\n|---|---|---|\n| BACnet MS/TP | RS-485 daisy chain | VFD as a BACnet device on an MS/TP trunk |\n| BACnet/IP | Ethernet | VFD with built-in Ethernet or communication card |\n| Modbus RTU | RS-485 daisy chain | VFD as a Modbus slave device |\n| Modbus TCP | Ethernet | VFD with Ethernet communication card |\n| Hardwired analog | 4–20 mA or 0–10 V | Analog output from controller to VFD speed input |\n\nMany VFDs require an optional communication card to support BACnet or Modbus over Ethernet. Verify the required card, firmware version, and configuration before ordering equipment.\n\n## Key Data Points\n\nA typical VFD integration exchanges the following data with the BAS:\n\n### Commands (BAS writes to VFD)\n\n| Point | Type | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| Start/Stop | Binary | Run command to the drive |\n| Speed Reference | Analog (0–100% or Hz) | Desired speed or frequency |\n| Fault Reset | Binary (momentary) | Clear a latched fault condition |\n\n### Status and Feedback (BAS reads from VFD)\n\n| Point | Type | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| Running Status | Binary | Drive is actively running |\n| Speed Feedback | Analog (0–100% or Hz) | Actual operating speed |\n| Motor Current | Analog (Amps) | Measured motor current draw |\n| Motor Power | Analog (kW) | Calculated or measured power consumption |\n| Output Frequency | Analog (Hz) | Actual output frequency |\n| Fault Status | Binary or multi-state | Active fault indication |\n| Fault Code | Integer | Manufacturer-specific fault identifier |\n| Drive Temperature | Analog (degrees) | Drive heatsink or internal temperature |\n| Runtime Hours | Integer | Accumulated running time |\n\nThe specific register addresses (Modbus) or object instances (BACnet) vary by VFD manufacturer and model. Always reference the manufacturer''s communication manual for the exact data map.\n\n## Command Ownership and Safety\n\nCommand ownership is the most critical design decision in a VFD integration. The BAS, the VFD keypad, a local Hand-Off-Auto (HOA) switch, and possibly other systems can all issue start/stop and speed commands. Conflicting commands can cause unexpected equipment behavior.\n\n### Design Rules\n\n- **Define a single command source priority scheme.** The VFD''s configuration determines which source has authority. Common schemes:\n  - HOA switch in HAND: local override, VFD runs at a fixed speed\n  - HOA switch in OFF: VFD is stopped regardless of network command\n  - HOA switch in AUTO: BAS has command authority via the network\n- **Hardware safeties must not depend on the network.** Freeze protection, high-pressure cutouts, and smoke interlock contacts should be hardwired directly to the VFD''s safety input terminals, not implemented solely through BACnet or Modbus commands.\n- **Define fault-reset behavior.** Some faults (overcurrent, ground fault) require investigation before resetting. The BAS should not auto-reset faults without limits. A common approach is to allow a configurable number of auto-resets (for example, 3) within a time window before requiring manual intervention.\n\n### Communication Loss Behavior\n\nConfigure the VFD''s response to communication loss:\n\n| Option | Behavior | When to Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stop on timeout | VFD ramps down and stops after communication timeout | Most HVAC applications |\n| Hold last speed | VFD continues at last commanded speed | Applications where stopping is worse than running at last speed |\n| Go to preset speed | VFD runs at a preconfigured safe speed | Critical ventilation or process applications |\n\nThe communication timeout is configured in the VFD (typically 5–30 seconds). Document this setting in the project sequence of operations.\n\n## BACnet VFD Integration\n\nWhen the VFD supports BACnet (MS/TP or IP):\n\n- The VFD appears as a BACnet device with its own device instance number.\n- Speed command is typically an Analog Output or Analog Value object on the VFD.\n- Start/stop command is typically a Binary Output or Binary Value object.\n- The BAS controller writes commands at the appropriate BACnet priority level.\n- Speed feedback, current, power, and fault status are readable BACnet objects.\n\nVerify the VFD''s BACnet Protocol Implementation Conformance Statement (PICS) to confirm which objects and services are supported.\n\n## Modbus VFD Integration\n\nWhen the VFD uses Modbus RTU or TCP:\n\n- The BAS controller (or a gateway) acts as the Modbus master; the VFD is a slave.\n- Speed command is written to a holding register (the specific register varies by manufacturer).\n- Start/stop command is typically a coil or a control word in a holding register.\n- Speed feedback, current, power, and faults are read from input registers or holding registers.\n- Scaling factors (e.g., speed as 0–10000 representing 0.00–100.00%) are defined in the manufacturer''s Modbus register map.\n\nCommon Modbus VFD integration issues:\n\n| Issue | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Drive does not respond to speed command | VFD not configured for network control source | Change VFD parameter for speed reference source to communication |\n| Drive does not start | VFD not configured for network start command | Change VFD parameter for run command source to communication |\n| Speed reads as wrong value | Scaling factor mismatch | Apply correct scaling per register map |\n| Intermittent communication | RS-485 wiring or termination | Check wiring, termination, baud rate, parity |\n\n## Commissioning Verification\n\nFor each VFD in the system:\n\n1. Confirm the VFD is configured for network control (speed reference and run command from communication).\n2. Verify HOA switch operation: HAND runs the drive locally, OFF stops the drive, AUTO gives control to the BAS.\n3. Command the drive to start from the BAS and verify the running status feedback matches.\n4. Command the drive to multiple speed setpoints (0%, 25%, 50%, 75%, 100%) and verify feedback tracks the command.\n5. Verify fault code reading by inducing a safe fault condition (if possible) or reading a stored fault.\n6. Test communication loss behavior: disconnect the network cable and verify the VFD responds as configured (stop, hold, or preset speed).\n7. Verify hardwired safeties operate independently of the network (e.g., close the smoke interlock contact and confirm the VFD stops regardless of BAS command state).\n8. Confirm motor current and power readings are plausible by comparing to nameplate values under load.\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135-2020: BACnet](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [Modbus Organization - Modbus Application Protocol Specification](https://modbus.org/specs.php)\n- [NEMA MG 1: Motors and Generators (motor nameplate and rating standards)](https://www.nema.org/standards/view/nema-mg-1-motors-and-generators)\n- [BACnet International - BACnet Testing Laboratories (BTL) Listed Products](https://bacnetinternational.org/btl/)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

