-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 6: BACnet COV Subscriptions
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'BACnet Change of Value (COV) Subscriptions: Configuration and Troubleshooting',
  'bacnet-cov-subscriptions-configuration-troubleshooting',
  'Reference guide for BACnet COV subscriptions, covering subscription types, increment configuration, lifetime management, and troubleshooting missed notifications in building automation systems.',
  E'# BACnet Change of Value (COV) Subscriptions: Configuration and Troubleshooting\n\nBACnet Change of Value (COV) subscriptions allow a client device to receive automatic notifications when a property value changes, rather than repeatedly polling the device on a fixed interval. When configured correctly, COV reduces network traffic and provides faster response to changes. When configured poorly, it can cause missed updates, network storms, or stale data.\n\n## How COV Works\n\nThe COV mechanism as defined in ASHRAE Standard 135 works as follows:\n\n1. A **subscribing client** (for example, a supervisor or front-end) sends a SubscribeCOV or SubscribeCOVProperty request to a **serving device** (for example, a field controller).\n2. The serving device acknowledges the subscription and begins monitoring the specified object.\n3. When the monitored property changes by more than the configured increment (or any change for binary objects), the serving device sends a COV notification to the subscriber.\n4. The subscription remains active for a configured lifetime. When the lifetime expires, the serving device stops sending notifications unless the client re-subscribes.\n\n## Subscription Types\n\nASHRAE 135 defines two subscription types:\n\n| Type | Service | What Is Monitored |\n|---|---|---|\n| SubscribeCOV | SubscribeCOV | Default COV-reportable properties of the object (typically Present_Value and Status_Flags) |\n| SubscribeCOVProperty | SubscribeCOVProperty | A specific property of the object, with a client-defined COV increment |\n\nSubscribeCOV is simpler and more widely supported. SubscribeCOVProperty provides finer control but requires the serving device to support the additional service.\n\n## COV Increment\n\nFor analog objects (Analog Input, Analog Output, Analog Value), the COV increment defines the minimum change in the present value that triggers a notification. For example:\n\n| Object | Present Value | COV Increment | Notification Triggers When |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Analog Input (temperature) | 72.0 degF | 0.5 | Value changes to 72.5 or 71.5 or beyond |\n| Analog Output (valve position) | 50.0% | 1.0 | Value changes to 51.0% or 49.0% or beyond |\n\nFor binary objects (Binary Input, Binary Output, Binary Value), any state change triggers a notification. The COV increment does not apply.\n\nThe COV increment is a property of the object on the serving device (COV_Increment property). The subscribing client can also specify an increment in the SubscribeCOVProperty request, which may override the object''s default.\n\n### Choosing the COV Increment\n\nThe increment should be small enough to capture meaningful changes but large enough to avoid excessive notifications:\n\n| Point Type | Suggested COV Increment | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Space temperature | 0.5 degF (0.3 degC) | Captures comfort-relevant changes |\n| Duct temperature | 1.0 degF (0.5 degC) | Process monitoring granularity |\n| Valve/damper position | 2.0% | Captures significant position changes |\n| Energy meter (kW) | 1.0 kW or 1% of range | Captures load changes |\n| Humidity | 2.0% RH | Captures meaningful shifts |\n| Pressure | 0.1 in. WC | Captures duct pressure changes |\n\nSetting the COV increment to zero on analog objects generates a notification for every tiny change, which can flood the network. Avoid this in production.\n\n## Subscription Lifetime\n\nEach COV subscription has a lifetime, specified in seconds by the subscriber:\n\n- When the lifetime expires, the serving device removes the subscription and stops sending notifications.\n- The subscriber must re-subscribe before the lifetime expires to maintain continuous coverage.\n- A lifetime value of 0 means the subscription does not expire (the device sends notifications indefinitely, or until the device restarts).\n\n### Lifetime Management Best Practices\n\n| Approach | Lifetime | Re-subscribe Interval | Trade-off |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Short lifetime | 300–600 seconds | Before expiry (e.g., at 80% of lifetime) | More network overhead from re-subscribes, but automatic cleanup of stale subscriptions |\n| Long lifetime | 3600–28800 seconds | Before expiry | Less re-subscribe overhead, but stale subscriptions persist longer if the subscriber crashes |\n| Infinite lifetime (0) | Never expires | Not needed | Simplest, but subscriptions persist until device restart; can accumulate orphans |\n\nMost BAS platforms handle re-subscription automatically. Verify that the platform''s COV subscription manager sends renewal requests before the lifetime expires.\n\n## Confirmed vs Unconfirmed Notifications\n\nCOV notifications can be sent as:\n\n| Type | Reliability | Network Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Confirmed | Subscriber must acknowledge each notification; device retries on failure | Higher (acknowledgment traffic) |\n| Unconfirmed | Fire-and-forget; no acknowledgment required | Lower, but notifications can be lost without detection |\n\nThe subscriber specifies whether it wants confirmed or unconfirmed notifications in the subscription request.\n\nFor critical monitoring points (alarms, safety status), use confirmed notifications. For informational trending or display updates, unconfirmed notifications are often acceptable.\n\n## Device Support Considerations\n\nNot all BACnet devices support COV subscriptions. Support depends on:\n\n- The device''s BACnet Implementation Conformance Statement (PICS) listing SubscribeCOV or SubscribeCOVProperty as a supported service.\n- The number of simultaneous COV subscriptions the device supports. Many field controllers have limited subscription capacity (for example, 20–100 active subscriptions).\n- Whether the device supports COV on the specific object types you need.\n\nWhen the subscription limit is reached, additional subscription requests will be rejected. Plan the number of COV subscriptions per device against the device''s documented capacity.\n\n## COV vs Polling: When to Use Each\n\n| Scenario | Recommended Approach | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Values that change frequently and unpredictably | COV with appropriate increment | Captures changes faster than polling |\n| Values that rarely change (setpoints, mode) | COV | Eliminates unnecessary polling traffic |\n| Devices that do not support COV | Polling | COV is not available |\n| Large number of points on a device with low COV limit | Polling for overflow points | Device cannot handle all subscriptions |\n| Critical control loops requiring guaranteed updates | Polling with short interval | Polling provides deterministic update rate |\n\nA common hybrid approach: use COV for status and alarm points (where timely notification matters) and polling for trend data (where a fixed interval is appropriate).\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| No notifications after subscribing | Device does not support COV, or subscription was rejected | Check device PICS; verify subscription was acknowledged |\n| Notifications stop after a period | Subscription lifetime expired and re-subscribe failed | Verify re-subscription logic; check lifetime parameter |\n| Excessive notifications flooding the network | COV increment set to zero or too small | Increase the COV increment to a practical value |\n| Stale values on the front-end | Unconfirmed notifications lost on a congested network | Switch to confirmed notifications or add fallback polling |\n| Device runs out of subscription slots | Too many subscriptions for the device capacity | Reduce subscription count; use polling for lower-priority points |\n| Notifications received but values seem delayed | Device batches COV notifications on a timer | Check device COV notification behavior in the vendor documentation |\n\n## Fallback Polling\n\nEven when COV is the primary update mechanism, a fallback polling interval (for example, every 5–15 minutes) provides a safety net. If a COV notification is missed or the subscription lapses, the fallback poll ensures the front-end eventually gets an updated value. Most BAS platforms support configuring both COV and fallback polling simultaneously.\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135-2020: BACnet - A Data Communication Protocol for Building Automation and Control Networks (Clause 13: COV Services)](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [BACnet International - BACnet Overview](https://bacnet.org/bacnet-overview/)\n- [BACnet International - BACnet Testing Laboratories (BTL) Product Listings](https://bacnetinternational.org/btl/)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

